## 22. Documentation Requirements for AI Projects

AI-enabled projects should document their behavior and limitations sufficiently for others to understand and evaluate them.

Depending on the project, documentation may include:

### Project Information

- Project purpose.
- Intended users.
- Intended use cases.
- Out-of-scope uses.
- AI functionality.
- Current maturity and status.

### Model Information

- Model name.
- Provider or source.
- Version or identifier.
- Relevant configuration.
- Important limitations.

### Data Information

- Data sources.
- Data classification.
- Processing purpose.
- Retention considerations.
- External processing dependencies.
- Data-use restrictions.

### System Information

- Architecture.
- Retrieval components.
- Tool integrations.
- Permission boundaries.
- Human oversight mechanisms.
- External dependencies.

### Evaluation Information

- Testing approach.
- Evaluation criteria.
- Results.
- Known failure modes.
- Limitations.
- Reproducibility constraints.

### User Information

Where relevant:

- AI disclosure.
- Expected behavior.
- Important limitations.
- Privacy information.
- Human review conditions.
- Available support or reporting channels.

Not every project requires a separate technical dossier.

The documentation level should remain proportionate to the project's risk and complexity.

